This code implements a `DisjointPairCounter` class that provides a method to count the number of disjoint pairs in an integer array. It uses a Union-Find data structure with path compression and union by rank for efficient disjoint set operations. The function also integrates with an external database to store and retrieve previously processed arrays and their disjoint pair counts, allowing for faster processing of large arrays.

The `countDisjointPairs` method takes in an integer array as input and returns the number of disjoint pairs found in it. It can optionally remove duplicates from the array before counting the disjoint pairs. The method also logs the start and end times of each operation, along with any errors encountered during execution.

The `unionFind` class provides a data structure for performing union-find operations efficiently. It uses a map to store the parents and ranks of elements in the Union-Find tree. The `union` method unions two elements by finding their roots and updating the parent map accordingly, while the `find` method finds the root of an element by traversing up the tree until it reaches a non-parent element.

