The `SerializationUtils` class provides two methods for deserializing a byte array into an object of a specified class: `deserialize()` and `deserializeNested()`. The `deserialize()` method supports deserialization of a single object, while the `deserializeNested()` method supports deserialization of multiple nested objects.

The `CLASS_CACHE` is a cache for frequently used classes and their constructors. It uses an LRU (Least Recently Used) policy to evict old entries based on the least recently used class or constructor. The maximum number of cached classes and their constructors is limited by the `MAX_CACHE_SIZE` constant.

The `deserialize()` method first checks if the specified class is already in the cache. If it is, it returns a new instance of that class using its cached constructor. Otherwise, it tries to get the class definition from an external data source (e.g., REST API or database) and adds it to the cache before instantiating the class.

The `deserializeNested()` method also checks if the specified class is already in the cache. If it is, it returns a new instance of that class using its cached constructor. Otherwise, it tries to get the class definition from an external data source (e.g., REST API or database) and adds it to the cache before instantiating the class. It also supports deserialization of multiple nested objects by recursively calling itself for each nested object.

The `getClassFromExternalDataSource()` method is used to fetch class definitions from an external data source (e.g., REST API or database). This method handles rate limits and retries for failed requests, as well as caching the class definition in memory for future use.

To ensure thread safety and support concurrent deserialization requests, the `SerializationUtils` class uses synchronization mechanisms such as locks and blocking queues. These mechanisms help to prevent race conditions and ensure that only one thread can access the cache at a time.

Finally, the `SerializationUtils` class provides an interface for administrators to update the report templates and data sources dynamically without restarting the application. This allows for more flexibility and adaptability in the system, while still maintaining high performance and low latency.

