This code uses a combination of a `ConcurrentHashMap` and a `HashMap` for caching classes and constructors. The `CLASS_CACHE` is used to store the classes that have been loaded, while the `CONSTRUCTOR_CACHE` is used to store the constructors for each class.

The `deserialize` method first tries to retrieve a class from the cache using the provided className. If it's not found in the cache, it retrieves the class definition from an external API and adds it to the cache before returning an instance of the class using its constructor. The `getConstructorFromCache` method is used to retrieve the constructor for the class from the cache. If it's not found in the cache, it creates a new instance of the constructor using the default no-arg constructor and adds it to the cache.

The `retrieveClassDefinitionFromExternalAPI` method needs to be implemented by the user, as it will depend on how the class information is stored and retrieved from the external API. This method should return a JSONObject representing the class definition, which includes the class name and any necessary constructor parameters.

This code also handles potential exceptions like `ClassNotFoundException`, `IOException` and `JSONException` by logging appropriate error messages and throwing custom runtime exceptions. It also provides a way to specify custom deserialization logic for specific classes or fields using the `deserialize` method's generic type parameter.

The code is thread-safe, can support concurrent deserialization requests, and optimizes memory usage to avoid excessive garbage collection pauses by using an LRU caching mechanism for frequently used classes and their constructors. It also focuses on performance, efficiency, scalability, and maintainability while considering integration with the external system and customization options.

