**Handle HTTP POST Requests for Web Application**

To handle HTTP POST requests for a web application, we can use C# as the programming language. We will create an asynchronous function that accepts parameters for the page section ID, element ID, and HTML content. The function will validate the input IDs, check user permissions, and update the element's HTML content using a service class.
```csharp
async Task HandleHttpPostRequestAsync(int pageSectionId, int elementId, string htmlContent)
{
    // Validate the input IDs.
    if (pageSectionId < 0 || elementId < 0)
        throw new ArgumentException("Invalid input ID");

    // Check user permissions.
    if (!UserHasPermission(pageSectionId, elementId))
        throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("You do not have permission to update this element.");

    // Use a service class to update the element's HTML content.
    var service = new ResourceService();
    await service.UpdateElementHtmlContentAsync(pageSectionId, elementId, htmlContent);
}
```
**Implement Comprehensive Error Handling**

To implement comprehensive error handling for various failure scenarios, we can use C#'s `try-catch` block to catch any exceptions that may occur during the process. We will also log any errors that occur using a logging framework like NLog or Serilog.
```csharp
async Task HandleHttpPostRequestAsync(int pageSectionId, int elementId, string htmlContent)
{
    try
    {
        // Validate the input IDs.
        if (pageSectionId < 0 || elementId < 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Invalid input ID");

        // Check user permissions.
        if (!UserHasPermission(pageSectionId, elementId))
            throw new UnauthorizedAccessException("You do not have permission to update this element.");

        // Use a service class to update the element's HTML content.
        var service = new ResourceService();
        await service.UpdateElementHtmlContentAsync(pageSectionId, elementId, htmlContent);
    }
    catch (Exception e)
    {
        logger.Error($"Error handling HTTP POST request: {e}");

        // Return an error response.
        return new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.InternalServerError)
        {
            Content = new StringContent("An unexpected error occurred.")
        };
    }
}
