To process real-time sensor data streams from IoT devices, you can use a distributed caching layer like Apache Ignite or Redis to store the data temporarily while it is being processed. The following code demonstrates how to integrate the microservice with an external KMS for key management and a distributed caching layer:
```java
import com.google.gson.JsonObject;
import io.jsonwebtoken.JwtBuilder;
import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import io.jsonwebtoken.SignatureAlgorithm;
import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.util.Map;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import org.apache.ignite.Ignite;
import org.apache.ignite.IgniteCache;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.Deserializer;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class JwtTokenService {
  private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(JwtTokenService.class);

  @Autowired
  private KeyPair keyPair;

  @Autowired
  private IgniteCache<String, String> cache;

  public String generateToken(Map<String, Object> claims) {
    JsonObject header = new JsonObject();
    header.addProperty("alg", "RS256");

    JwtBuilder builder = Jwts.builder()
        .setHeader(header)
        .claims(claims);

    String jwt = builder.signWith(SignatureAlgorithm.forName("SHA-256"), keyPair.getPrivate())
        .compact();

    return jwt;
  }

  public void processSensorData(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
    JsonObject payload = new JsonObject(record.value());
    String token = payload.getString("token");

    if (isValidToken(token)) {
      cache.put(token, record.key());
    } else {
      logger.warn("Received invalid token from sensor device: " + token);
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation uses the `IgniteCache` interface provided by Apache Ignite to store the data temporarily while it is being processed. The `ConsumerRecord` object is used to represent a message received from a Kafka topic, and the `processSensorData()` method is called whenever a new message is received. The method validates the token in the message using the `isValidToken()` method, and if it is valid, it stores the data in the cache under the token.

To optimize disk I/O operations and ensure robust concurrent data processing capabilities, you can use a distributed database system like Apache Kafka or Apache Cassandra to store the data. The following code demonstrates how to integrate the microservice with a distributed database system:
```java
import com.google.gson.JsonObject;
import io.jsonwebtoken.JwtBuilder;
import io.jsonwebtoken.Jwts;
import io.jsonwebtoken.SignatureAlgorithm;
import java.security.KeyPair;
import java.util.Map;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import org.apache.cassandra.service.CassandraService;
import org.apache.kafka.clients.consumer.ConsumerRecord;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class JwtTokenService {
  private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(JwtTokenService.class);

  @Autowired
  private KeyPair keyPair;

  @Autowired
  private CassandraService cassandra;

  public String generateToken(Map<String, Object> claims) {
    JsonObject header = new JsonObject();
    header.addProperty("alg", "RS256");

    JwtBuilder builder = Jwts.builder()
        .setHeader(header)
        .claims(claims);

    String jwt = builder.signWith(SignatureAlgorithm.forName("SHA-256"), keyPair.getPrivate())
        .compact();

    return jwt;
  }

  public void processSensorData(ConsumerRecord<String, String> record) {
    JsonObject payload = new JsonObject(record.value());
    String token = payload.getString("token");

    if (isValidToken(token)) {
      cassandra.insertIntoTable(token, record.key(), record.value());
    } else {
      logger.warn("Received invalid token from sensor device: " + token);
    }
  }
}
```
This implementation uses the `CassandraService` interface provided by Apache Cassandra to store the data in a distributed database. The `insertIntoTable()` method is called whenever new data is received, and it stores the data in a table based on the token.

