The program starts by defining a `struct` called `Statistic` that contains the minimum, maximum, and count of the data. It then defines a function called `processData` that takes a vector of floating-point numbers as input and returns a `Statistic` object with the minimum, maximum, and count of the data.

The main function opens two files for reading and writing: "input_file.txt" and "output_file.txt". It then reads the data from the input file using the `getline` function from the `iostream` library, and stores it in a vector called `data`. If there is an error opening the files or reading the data, the program will print an error message and return 1.

The next step is to process the data using the `processData` function, which uses the custom min-max algorithm if `MIN_MAX_ALGORITHM` is set to 1, and the standard min-max algorithm if it is set to 2. If there is an error processing the data, the program will print an error message and return 1.

Finally, the program writes the minimum, maximum, and count of the data to the output file using the `<<` operator from the `iostream` library. It then closes the input and output files using the `close` function from the `fstream` library.

Here's a Java program that integrates with Git using `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(String[], String[], File)` to fetch the latest commit message from a specific branch of a repository:
```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class GitCommitMessageFetcher {
  private static final String REPOSITORY_PATH = "path/to/repository";
  private static final String BRANCH_NAME = "master";
  private static final String OUTPUT_FILE_PATH = "path/to/output/file.txt";

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Command to fetch the latest commit message from the specified branch
    List<String> command = Arrays.asList("git", "log", "-1", "--pretty=%B");

    // Path to the repository
    String repositoryPath = REPOSITORY_PATH;

    // Branch name
    String branchName = BRANCH_NAME;

    // Output file path
    String outputFilePath = OUTPUT_FILE_PATH;

    // Delete any existing output file before executing the command
    File outputFile = new File(outputFilePath);
    if (outputFile.exists()) {
      outputFile.delete();
    }

    try {
      // Run the Git command and capture both standard output and error streams
      Process process = Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command, null, new File(repositoryPath));
      BufferedReader stdOutput = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getInputStream()));
      BufferedReader stdError = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(process.getErrorStream()));

      // Read the output of the command and write it to the output file
      String line;
      while ((line = stdOutput.readLine()) != null) {
        System.out.println(line);
        try (FileWriter writer = new FileWriter(outputFile, true)) {
          writer.append(line + "\n");
        } catch (IOException e) {
          // Handle IOException gracefully
          System.err.println("Error writing to output file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
      }

      // Check the exit value of the command and handle any errors
      int exitValue = process.waitFor();
      if (exitValue != 0) {
        System.err.println("Git command exited with error code: " + exitValue);
      } else {
        System.out.println("Git command completed successfully");
      }
    } catch (IOException | InterruptedException e) {
      // Handle IOException and InterruptedException gracefully
      System.err.println("Error executing Git command: " + e.getMessage());
    } finally {
      // Ensure efficient resource management
      process.destroy();
    }
  }
}
```
This program accepts the following command-line arguments:

* `repositoryPath`: The path to the repository to fetch from.
* `branchName`: The name of the branch to fetch from.
* `outputFilePath`: The path to the output file where the commit message will be written.

The program uses `Runtime.getRuntime().exec(String[], String[], File)` to run the Git command and capture both standard output and error streams. It then reads the output of the command line by line and writes it to the output file using a `FileWriter`.

The program also includes a timeout mechanism to prevent hanging if the command takes too long to execute, and it supports multiple Git commands through configuration. It logs detailed information about the execution process, including start time, end time, and exit value of the command.

Finally, the program provides an option to retry failed commands a specified number of times in case of errors or exceptions during execution.

