### Solution Explanation

In this solution, we first define a `S3Client` class that wraps the AWS SDK for C++ and provides a simple interface for interacting with an S3 bucket. The class has methods for uploading files to the bucket, downloading files from the bucket, and listing all of the objects in the bucket.

We then define a `MetadataCache` class that caches file metadata (such as the file size and last modification time) for a given set of files. This cache is used to avoid unnecessary lookups in the AWS SDK when we already have this information locally.

The `S3Client` and `MetadataCache` classes are both instantiated with an `Aws::S3::S3Client` object, which is created using the AWS SDK for C++. This allows us to use the AWS SDK for C++ to interact with the S3 bucket.

In the `main()` function, we first create a `S3Client` object and use it to upload the file "testfile" to the S3 bucket. We then retrieve the metadata for this file using the `getObjectMetadata()` method of the `S3Client` class. This returns an `Aws::S3::Model::HeadObjectResult` object, which contains information such as the file size and last modification time of the file.

We then use a `std::unordered_map` to keep track of the metadata for each file in the S3 bucket. We add a new entry to this map with the filename as the key and the `Aws::S3::Model::HeadObjectResult` object as the value.

Next, we iterate over all of the files in the S3 bucket using the `listObjects()` method of the `S3Client` class. For each file, we retrieve its metadata from the map that we created earlier and print out some information about it (such as the file size and last modification time). We also check if the file is newer than the previous version, and if so, update the previous version with the newest version.

Finally, we use the `deleteObject()` method of the `S3Client` class to delete any files in the S3 bucket that are older than a certain age (in this case, one day).

