To handle errors gracefully, you can use error handling functions provided by OpenSSL, such as `ERR_error_string` to retrieve a human-readable error message and `ERR_get_error` to obtain the error code. You can also consider using techniques such as error injection or fault injection to test the robustness of your implementation.

To ensure the solution is scalable and can handle concurrent key derivations and cipher initializations, you can use techniques such as load balancing or distributed computing to distribute the workload across multiple machines or processes. You can also consider using lock-free data structures or other thread-safe data structures to minimize contention and improve performance.

