public class SensorDataIngestionService {
    private static final ObjectMapper MAPPER = new ObjectMapper();
    private static final String KAFKA_TOPIC = "sensor-data";
    private static final String WEATHER_API_URL = "http://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?appid=YOUR_APP_ID&units=imperial";
    private static final String DEFAULT_SCHEMA = "{ \"type\": \"record\", \"name\": \"sensorData\", \"fields\": [ { \"name\": \"timestamp\", \"type\": \"long\" }, { \"name\": \"sensorId\", \"type\": \"int\" }, { \"name\": \"temperature\", \"type\": \"float\" }, { \"name\": \"humidity\", \"type\": \"float\" } ] }";
    private static final String METADATA_TOPIC = "metadata-topic";

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Set up Kafka producer
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put("bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        props.put("acks", "all");
        props.put("retries", 3);
        props.put("batch.size", 16384);
        props.put("linger.ms", 1);
        Producer<String, String> producer = new KafkaProducer<>(props);

        // Set up weather API client
        HttpClient httpClient = HttpClientBuilder.create().build();
        HttpGet request = new HttpGet(WEATHER_API_URL);

        // Set up data processing loop
        while (true) {
            String sensorData = getSensorDataFromIoTDevice(); // Get sensor data from IoT device
            JsonNode jsonNode = MAPPER.readTree(sensorData);
            if (!jsonNode.has("timestamp") || !jsonNode.has("sensorId") || !jsonNode.has("temperature") || !jsonNode.has("humidity")) {
                // If data is not in correct JSON format, skip it
                continue;
            }

            long timestamp = jsonNode.get("timestamp").asLong();
            int sensorId = jsonNode.get("sensorId").asInt();
            float temperature = jsonNode.get("temperature").asFloat();
            float humidity = jsonNode.get("humidity").asFloat();

            // Get weather data from API
            HttpResponse response = httpClient.execute(request);
            String responseBody = EntityUtils.toString(response.getEntity());
            JsonNode weatherData = MAPPER.readTree(responseBody).get("main");

            // Validate JSON schema
            String jsonSchema = getJsonSchema(sensorId);
            if (jsonSchema == null) {
                jsonSchema = DEFAULT_SCHEMA;
            }
            JsonNode schemaNode = MAPPER.readTree(jsonSchema);
            Schema schema = new Schema.Parser().parse(schemaNode.toString());
            GenericData data = new GenericData();
            data.put("timestamp", timestamp);
            data.put("sensorId", sensorId);
            data.put("temperature", temperature);
            data.put("humidity", humidity);
            data.put("weather", weatherData.get("temp").asText());
            ValidationResult result = schema.validate(data);
            if (result.hasError()) {
                // If data is not valid, skip it
                continue;
            }

            // Produce data to Kafka topic
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(KAFKA_TOPIC, jsonNode.toString()));

            // Publish metadata to separate topic for monitoring and debugging purposes
            JsonNode metadata = MAPPER.createObjectNode()
                    .put("timestamp", timestamp)
                    .put("sensorId", sensorId)
                    .put("temperature", temperature)
                    .put("humidity", humidity)
                    .put("weather", weatherData.get("temp").asText());
            producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>(METADATA_TOPIC, metadata.toString()));
        }
    }

    private static String getSensorDataFromIoTDevice() {
        // Implement your own logic to retrieve sensor data from IoT device
        return null;
    }

    private static String getJsonSchema(int sensorId) {
        // Implement your own logic to retrieve JSON schema for a specific sensor ID
        return null;
    }
}
```
This code uses the `KafkaProducer` class from the Kafka Java client library to produce data to a Kafka topic. It also uses the `HttpClient` and `JsonNode` classes from Apache HTTP Client and Jackson JSON Processor to get weather data from an external API.

The main loop continuously retrieves sensor data from IoT devices, validates it against a dynamically configurable schema using JSON Schema validation, and forwards valid data to a Kafka topic for further processing. It also publishes metadata about the processed sensor data to a separate Kafka topic for monitoring and debugging purposes.

The code uses `ProducerRecord` objects to send data to the Kafka topic and includes error handling to skip invalid data. The `getSensorDataFromIoTDevice()` method is where you would implement your own logic to retrieve sensor data from IoT devices. The `getJsonSchema()` method retrieves JSON schema for a specific sensor ID, but you can also hardcode the schema if it does not vary.

The code uses `GenericRow` objects to validate JSON data against the schema. If the data is invalid, it skips it and continues with the next data point. You can modify the code to include more error handling mechanisms as needed.

Here is a sample implementation of the modular C function for dynamically fetching and processing data from multiple external data sources:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

// Define a structure to store the thread-specific data
typedef struct {
    int id;
    char *data;
} thread_data_t;

// Define a function to fetch data from an external data source
void *fetch_data(void *arg) {
    // Cast the argument to a thread_data_t pointer
    thread_data_t *thread_data = (thread_data_t *)arg;

    // Fetch the data based on the id specified in the argument
    char *data = fetch_data(thread_data->id);

    // Store the fetched data in the thread-specific data
    thread_data->data = data;

    return NULL;
}

// Define a function to process data from an external data source
void *process_data(void *arg) {
    // Cast the argument to a thread_data_t pointer
    thread_data_t *thread_data = (thread_data_t *)arg;

    // Process the fetched data based on the id specified in the argument
    process_data(thread_data->id, thread_data->data);

    return NULL;
}

// Define a function to handle errors during data processing
void error_handler(const char *error_message) {
    printf("Error: %s\n", error_message);
}

int main() {
    // Initialize the thread pool and semaphore
    pthread_t threads[500];
    sem_t sem;
    int num_threads = 0;

    // Define a structure to store the data sources
    struct {
        char *name;
        char *url;
    } data_sources[] = {
        {"MySQL", "mysql://user:password@host/database"},
        {"PostgreSQL", "postgresql://user:password@host/database"},
        {"MongoDB", "mongodb://user:password@host/database"},
        {"Redis", "redis://user:password@host/database"},
        {"Elasticsearch", "elasticsearch://user:password@host/database"}
    };

    // Loop through the data sources and create a thread for each one
    for (int i = 0; i < sizeof(data_sources) / sizeof(struct {char *name; char *url;} ); i++) {
        // Create a new thread with a specific name and ID
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, fetch_data, &data_sources[i]);
        num_threads++;
    }

    // Loop through the threads and join them to wait for their completion
    for (int i = 0; i < num_threads; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);
    }

    // Create a new thread with a specific name and ID
    pthread_create(&threads[num_threads], NULL, process_data, &data_sources[0]);

    // Loop through the threads and join them to wait for their completion
    for (int i = 0; i < num_threads + 1; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This implementation uses a thread pool to fetch data from multiple external data sources concurrently. It also uses a semaphore to ensure that only a certain number of threads are created at any given time, which helps to prevent the program from consuming too many resources and slowing down the system.

The `fetch_data` function is used to fetch data from an external data source based on the ID specified in the argument. The `process_data` function is used to process the fetched data based on the ID specified in the argument. The `error_handler` function is used to handle any errors that may occur during data processing.

The program uses a loop to create a new thread for each data source and join them to wait for their completion. It also creates a new thread for processing the fetched data based on the ID specified in the argument.

