```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

#define MAX_THREADS 100
#define BUFFER_SIZE 1024

// Structure to hold the thread arguments
struct thread_args {
    int index;
};

// Function to be executed by each thread
void *process_integers(void *args) {
    struct thread_args *thread_arg = (struct thread_args *) args;

    // Get the starting and ending indices for this thread
    int start_index = thread_arg->index * BUFFER_SIZE;
    int end_index = start_index + BUFFER_SIZE;
    if (end_index > MAX_INTEGERS) {
        end_index = MAX_INTEGERS;
    }

    // Initialize the semaphore for this thread
    sem_t *sem = sem_open("integer_sem", O_CREAT, 0644, 1);

    // Loop through the integers in the buffer
    for (int i = start_index; i < end_index; i++) {
        // Wait for the semaphore to be available
        sem_wait(sem);

        // Check if the integer is prime or not
        int is_prime = check_prime(integers[i]);

        // If the integer is prime, print it to stdout
        if (is_prime) {
            printf("%d\n", integers[i]);
        }

        // Release the semaphore so that other threads can access the buffer
        sem_post(sem);
    }

    // Close the semaphore
    sem_close(sem);

    return NULL;
}

// Function to check if an integer is prime or not
int check_prime(int num) {
    // Check if the number is greater than 1
    if (num > 1) {
        // Loop from 2 to the square root of the number
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= num; i++) {
            // If the number is divisible by any number between 2 and the square root of the number, it is not prime
            if (num % i == 0) {
                return 0;
            }
        }
    } else {
        // The number is either 0 or 1, neither of which are prime
        return 0;
    }

    // If the number makes it to this point, it is prime
    return 1;
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Initialize the array for the integers
    int integers[MAX_INTEGERS];

    // Create a semaphore to control access to the buffer
    sem_t *sem = sem_open("integer_sem", O_CREAT, 0644, 1);

    // Initialize the thread pool
    pthread_t threads[MAX_THREADS];

    // Loop through each thread and create it
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; i++) {
        struct thread_args *thread_arg = malloc(sizeof(struct thread_args));
        thread_arg->index = i;
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, process_integers, (void *) thread_arg);
    }

    // Wait for all threads to finish
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);
    }

    // Close the semaphore
    sem_close(sem);

    return 0;
}
```
This code creates a thread pool of `MAX_THREADS` threads, each of which reads a chunk of integers from a shared buffer and checks if they are prime. The `process_integers()` function is executed by each thread, and it uses the semaphore to control access to the shared buffer.

The `main()` function creates the shared buffer and initializes the thread pool. It then loops through each thread and creates it, passing in the starting index for the buffer that should be used by that thread. The threads are joined at the end of the program to ensure that they have completed their work before the program exits.

The `check_prime()` function is a simple implementation of the Sieve of Eratosthenes, which checks if a number is prime or not. It uses a loop to check if any number between 2 and the square root of the number is divisible by it, and returns 0 if it finds a factor. If no factors are found, it returns 1, indicating that the number is likely prime.

This code should be able to handle increasing data sizes and high concurrency without issue. The semaphore is used to control access to the shared buffer, which ensures that only one thread can read from the buffer at a time and prevents race conditions. The `malloc()` function is used to allocate memory for the thread arguments, which allows the program to create as many threads as needed without exhausting the available memory.

To test this code, you could use a large dataset of integers, such as the first million prime numbers or the first billion prime numbers. You could also write unit tests to verify that the program is producing the correct output for various inputs and edge cases.

